## Document Object Model (DOM)

The **Document Object Model (DOM)** is a programming interface for HTML documents. It represents an HTML document as a **tree of objects or nodes**. JavaScript can use the DOM to access, modify, add or delete HTML elements and their content.

For example, JavaScript can use the DOM to change the text, style or attributes of an HTML element.

### Program to change "Wel come" to "Welcome to the University"

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>DOM Example</title>
</head>

<body>

    <p id="message">Wel come</p>

    <button onclick="changeText()">Change Text</button>

    <script>
        function changeText() {
            document.getElementById("message").innerHTML =
                "Welcome to the University.";
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
### Explanation

1. `<p id="message">Wel come</p>` creates a paragraph and gives it the ID **message**.
2. `document.getElementById("message")` finds the paragraph using its ID.
3. `.innerHTML` is used to change the content of the paragraph.
4. When the button is clicked, the `changeText()` function is called.
5. The paragraph changes from **"Wel come"** to **"Welcome to the University."**

### Output

**Before clicking the button:**

> Wel come

**After clicking the button:**

> Welcome to the University.
